@* Plain-language guide to the masking strategies, for users who aren't technical. Examples are illustrative. *@
<section id="strategies" class="mt-4" data-test="strategy-guide" style="max-width: 72rem">
    <h2 class="h4">What the masking strategies do</h2>
    <p>
        After you upload a file, each column gets a <strong>strategy</strong>: the rule for how its values are disguised.
        The tool suggests one for every column; you can change any of them before anything runs.
        Masking only works one way: masked values can't be turned back into the originals.
    </p>

    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-sm align-top">
            <thead class="table-light">
                <tr>
                    <th style="width: 9rem">Strategy</th>
                    <th>What it does</th>
                    <th style="width: 15rem">Example (before → after)</th>
                    <th style="width: 15rem">Good for</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Keep</strong></td>
                    <td>Leaves the column exactly as it is.</td>
                    <td><code>North</code> → <code>North</code></td>
                    <td>Information that isn't sensitive: regions, statuses, states, yes/no flags.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>HashId</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Swaps each code for a random-looking one with the same shape: same length, letters stay letters,
                        digits stay digits, dashes stay put. The same original code always becomes the same new code.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>CUST-00042</code> → <code>QZRT-07193</code></td>
                    <td>Customer IDs, account numbers, order numbers, any code that links records together.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Fake</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Replaces the value with a realistic but made-up one of the kind you choose: person name, first or last name,
                        company, hospital, email, phone, street address or city. The same original always gets the same fake.
                        Fake emails use example.com addresses, which can never receive mail.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>Jane Smith</code> → <code>Carlos Rivera</code></td>
                    <td>Names of people and organizations, contact details, addresses.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>ZipRemap</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Changes a ZIP code to another one in the same area (the first 3 digits stay the same), so regional
                        totals still add up. The new ZIP may not be a real mailing ZIP.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>02134</code> → <code>02187</code></td>
                    <td>ZIP / postal code columns (US only).</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Perturb</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Nudges each number up or down by a small random amount (by default up to 15%). The format, the sign,
                        the decimal places and zeros are kept, so totals and charts look about the same, just not exact.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>$1,250.00</code> → <code>$1,318.75</code></td>
                    <td>Amounts, prices, revenue, quantities, counts.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>DateShift</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Moves every date by the same random number of days (by default up to 30, earlier or later), so
                        gaps between dates and their order stay the same. The date format and any time of day are kept.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>03/05/2024</code> → <code>03/19/2024</code></td>
                    <td>Order dates, visit dates, any date that could help identify someone.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Redact</strong></td>
                    <td>Replaces every non-blank value with <code>[REDACTED]</code> (or text you choose).</td>
                    <td><code>Called about a refund…</code> → <code>[REDACTED]</code></td>
                    <td>Free-text notes and comments, or anything you don't need in the test data at all.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Lorem</strong></td>
                    <td>
                        Replaces text with placeholder words of exactly the same length, so reports and layouts look
                        the same without showing the real text.
                    </td>
                    <td><code>Called about a refund</code> → <code>Lorem ipsum dolor sit</code></td>
                    <td>Free text where the length matters, e.g. testing how a report's text boxes wrap.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="h6 mt-3">Good to know</h3>
    <ul class="small">
        <li><strong>Blank cells stay blank</strong> with every strategy, and the file keeps the same rows, columns and column names.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistent within a file:</strong> with HashId, Fake and ZipRemap, a value that appears many times is masked the same way every time, so counts and joins still work. A new upload gets different masked values.</li>
        <li><strong>Values that don't fit a strategy</strong> (for example "N/A" in an amount column) are replaced with <code>[REDACTED]</code>, never left as they were, and the report tells you how many.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="h6 mt-3">Other options on the review screen</h3>
    <dl class="small row">
        <dt class="col-sm-3">Linked to (another column)</dt>
        <dd class="col-sm-9">
            Ties a column to an ID column, such as linking Customer_Name to Customer_ID. Every row for the same customer then gets the
            same fake identity, and their fake name and email match. The tool suggests links automatically.
        </dd>
        <dt class="col-sm-3">Same mapping as (another column)</dt>
        <dd class="col-sm-9">
            Makes two columns mask identically, for example Bill-to and Ship-to customer, so the same customer looks the same in both.
        </dd>
        <dt class="col-sm-3">Per entity</dt>
        <dd class="col-sm-9">
            For Perturb and DateShift on a linked column: each customer gets their own adjustment, so all of one customer's
            amounts move together and all of their dates shift together.
        </dd>
        <dt class="col-sm-3">Recipes</dt>
        <dd class="col-sm-9">
            Saved column settings (never any data). If you upload a file with the same columns again, your choices are filled in for you.
        </dd>
    </dl>
</section>
